# Memory findings that are nginx's own, not this module's.  The valgrind job
# drives the module through ci/link.sh, which runs one foreground nginx
# (master_process off) end to end with curl and shuts it down with QUIT, so
# the pools are freed and whatever is left here belongs to nginx core.  No
# frame from ngx_link_func_module.c appears in either entry.
#
# Both are measured, not assumed.  The first, nginx's environment array, is
# the only definite loss an empty suppress file reports on a glibc build such
# as Rocky.  The second, the per-process connection and event arrays set up
# in ngx_event_process_init, is reported as a definite loss by the valgrind
# on the Ubuntu CI runner and not on Rocky, a difference in how the two
# classify memory still pointed at from process-exit state; it is nginx core
# setting up its event loop once and letting the process exit reclaim it.  If
# a later nginx brings a new finding, valgrind prints a full trace; add it
# then, with a name and a reason.

{
   nginx keeps the environment array until the process ends
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite
   fun:malloc
   fun:ngx_alloc
   fun:ngx_set_environment
   ...
}

{
   nginx per-process connection and event arrays, foreground single-process mode
   Memcheck:Leak
   match-leak-kinds: definite,indirect
   ...
   fun:ngx_event_process_init
   ...
}
